  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Roberto Cavalli grew up in Florence in a family shaped by painting and tailoring: his grandfather was an artist and his mother a seamstress. Art school led him towards textile design. He opened a small printing workshop, experimenting with applying patterns to finished knitwear rather than treating fabric simply as a background for a garment.\n\nLeather became another surface for that experimentation. Cavalli developed and patented a printing process for leather and suede, then presented printed leather eveningwear in Paris in 1970. This combination of surface decoration and unconventional materials helped turn his workshop practice into the foundation of a fashion house.",
    "html": "<p>Roberto Cavalli grew up in Florence in a family shaped by painting and tailoring: his grandfather was an artist and his mother a seamstress. Art school led him towards textile design. He opened a small printing workshop, experimenting with applying patterns to finished knitwear rather than treating fabric simply as a background for a garment.</p>\r\n<p>Leather became another surface for that experimentation. Cavalli developed and patented a printing process for leather and suede, then presented printed leather eveningwear in Paris in 1970. This combination of surface decoration and unconventional materials helped turn his workshop practice into the foundation of a fashion house.</p>\r\n",
